  "worked_solution": "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times a $2$ or $3$ is rolled.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=7$ rolls\n\n- the probability of getting a $2$ or $3$ on any roll of the die is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{2}{6}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(7,\\frac{1}{3}).$\n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the mean is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSo, the mean number of times we get a $2$ or a $3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =np \\\\  & =7\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
